1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
One sky. The Arabs saw — inns where the
Moon stays each night.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:11,000
Each night, the Moon shifts about 13°
east. In 27 or 28 nights, it circles the
sky.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:16,000
In Arabic, each night’s stop is a
manzil: a lodging.

4
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:25,000
Around Scorpius: a great scorpion, with
claws, a heart and a raised tail.

5
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:33,000
Then a flock of ostriches. Then
al-Baldah, ‘the empty place’: no bright
stars, so the Moon sleeps outdoors.

6
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:39,000
The inn signs reached Europe. Aldebaran
is al-Dabarān, ‘the follower’: it
follows the Pleiades.

7
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:42,000
Shaula: ‘the raised tail’.

8
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:50,000
The scorpion’s claws are now Libra:
Zubenelgenubi and Zubeneschamali,
‘southern claw’ and ‘northern claw’.

9
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:01,000
More than half of the 28 stations live
on in official IAU star names.

10
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:07,000
Antares: in China, a star of Xin, ‘the
Heart’. In Arabic, al-Qalb, ‘the heart’.

11
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Islamic months traditionally start at
the first thin crescent. Saudi Arabia’s
calendar: New Year on Sunday 6 June.

12
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,000
Look low in the west-northwest: a thin
crescent at the feet of Gemini.

13
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:26,000
Al-Ṣūfī’s Book of Fixed Stars, over
1,000 years old.

14
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:29,000
Follow for a new sky every week.

